cartridge "RED ALERT/T.BRANTNER/1987"

' RED ALERT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Red Alert" by Tony Brantner, Ahoy!
' September 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, RED ALERT.ML at $C000,
' 2,150 bytes, started by a two-line BASIC loader; author from the issue's
' contents page). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/redalert-ml.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a starry night over six towns on dark hills, the missile
' base on a mound in the middle, warheads with trails as smooth sprites,
' growing blasts, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: enemy warheads fall on six towns and your base. Move the
' sight and press a button: an interceptor flies from the base to the
' sight and bursts there; every warhead caught in the burst is destroyed.
' A warhead that lands flattens its town, or the base (no more firing
' until the wave is over). After each wave the towns left score 100 each.
' The game ends when a wave is over and no town is left.
'
' From the original (orig/): seven slots, the interceptor in slot 0 and
' six warheads; a warhead starts above the screen at a random place and
' aims at one of eight targets (the six towns, the base twice), moving
' (target - start) / 256 a step, so it lands after exactly 256 steps; a
' step every (24 - wave) x 2 passes of the C64's loop (about 26 passes a
' frame: 13 / (24 - wave) steps a frame here, StepAcc); 16-23 warheads a
' wave (random); the interceptor flies the same time to any sight
' position, then its burst destroys the warheads near its middle (only the
' interceptor's burst does, not the warheads'); wave x 10 points a
' warhead, 100 a town left after the wave, a spare town for every 10,000
' points (counted once a wave, rebuilds a lost town at the next wave); the
' waves stop getting harder at 20; one interceptor at a time, the button
' may be held; the sight's limits; six-digit score and high score.
'
' Liberties: the field is scaled to the ColecoVision screen (the sight's
' C64 speed of about 1.6 pixels a frame is 1 pixel here, and 2 after
' holding the stick for 10 frames); the C64 launched every free slot on
' the same step (all six together): here a warhead is launched at most
' every 32 steps, so they come at different heights (fewer than five on a
' line) and keep coming steadily; the step rate stops rising at wave 14
' (13 / 10 steps a frame) where the C64 kept going to 13 / 4 at wave 20,
' too fast to aim at for a person; the interceptor takes 10 frames (as on
' the C64) and the burst lasts 18 frames growing to a radius of 8 pixels
' and back (C64 about 10 frames, 10 C64 pixels square), so the burst can
' be seen; collisions on the warhead's tip; a town's beep as it is
' counted and a jingle for a spare town; GAME OVER then the title (the
' C64 went back to PRESS FIRE BUTTON TO START).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the sight, either button
' fires. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers (the test bot replays them).
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs 25 (sprite count) removed 2026-11-01.

